Subject: [PATCH v3 0/6] Input: aw86927 - add AW86937 support and fix the AW8693X
 layout

*** AI-assistance disclosure ***
This cover letter and the six patches below were prepared with the help
of an LLM coding assistant.  The author reviewed every line, ran every
gate quoted in this letter himself, and takes full responsibility for
the content.

This series adds support for the Awinic AW86937 LRA haptic IC on top of
the existing AW86927/AW86938 driver, fixes two register definitions that
are wrong on the AW8693x parts, and stops playback over suspend/shutdown.

Patch 1 fixes the 7 V boost reference value that the AW86938 support
introduced (0x11 programs 9 V, not 7 V).  Patch 2 widens the AW86938
boost mode mask: the field is two bits wide at 6:5, so the existing
one-bit mask leaves bit 6 untouched when the driver selects bypass.
Patch 3 documents the awinic,aw86937 compatible as another member of the
AW86927 fallback group; detection stays on the chip ID, so no new
of_device_id entry is added.  Patch 4 adds the chip ID and init path.
Patch 5 handles the AW8693x register layout differences (auto-boost bit,
two-bit boost mode, VBAT mode and the two-step stop handshake).  Patch 6
stops playback over suspend and shutdown, and drains the work item on
unbind.

What changed in v3
==================

Both findings of the 2026-11-17 sashiko review are addressed in patch 6;
patches 1-5 only differ from v2 in the series numbering and the resulting
hunk line offsets, their content is unchanged.

  - [High] unbind could queue play_work after the drain.  The devm reset
    action now calls disable_work_sync() before asserting reset, which
    both drains a work item queued by input_flush_device() while the
    input device is unregistered and keeps further events from queueing
    it.  play_work is initialised earlier, before the devm action is
    registered, so the action can be released on every error path.
  - [Low] the commit message and the frame-callback comment described
    both paths as "stop playback, drain and park in reset".  They now
    state what each path does: suspend marks the device suspended,
    drains the work and puts the chip into standby; shutdown drains the
    work and asserts reset; unbind and probe failure do the same through
    the devm action before the registers are released.

The AW86937 has no hardware in hand: the register data comes from the
vendor driver and the part datasheet, and the commit message does not
claim otherwise.  No runtime testing is claimed for v3 either; the race
described above is reasoned from the input core and devres ordering.

